<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajuvula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1st century BCE – 1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajuvula was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> ruler, styled great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a>, who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around the turn of the 1st century BCE and the 1st century CE. He issued coins copying those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> kings. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura-lion-capital">Mathura Lion Capital</a>, an inscribed sculpture in Kharoshthi now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, records gifts to the Buddhist community by his chief queen.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajuvula.jpg" alt="Rajuvula" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Black and white images: Beglar, Joseph David 1875 photograph Public Domain British Museum Online Color photograph: Bi... · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kambojika_statue_Mathura.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
